 | modsonline.test.js | 10 | MODS-ONLINE-2 - EVERY MOD IS THE PLAYER'S ONLINE, AND THE GROUND IS THE ROOM'S (2026-09-22, Mac: "Is it possible to allow all mods to be toggled on and off for online?" and then "So all mods can now be toggled?"). MODS-ONLINE freed eight mods and left eight forced because they SOUNDED like world state; this file holds the reading that freed the rest and overturns one of that pass's own rulings. The port is already owner-authoritative everywhere a mod could disagree: the striker rolls the damage and the host applies the number without recomputing, a foe's stats are minted where it SPAWNS and a peer steps a puppet, a corpse's pile is the owner's word, and a blow is mitigated where it LANDS - damageShieldPool inside damageFoe for a foe and inside hurtPlayer for me - so the Shield Widget's block is the DEFENDER's own. None of that crosses as a rule; it crosses as a result. ONE THING IS THE FLOOR: Basic Roads rewrites terrain heights, so `Enabled` and `SmoothRoads` are the room's and were the only two switches the lane forced in the whole shelf (three since LANDFORM3, below - AUDIT LANDFORMS D13). THE THIRD PIN MEASURES THAT rather than asserting it - the real smoother is run over a road bed (the heights move) and then over every tile the river and stream tables can write (the heights come back byte-identical), which is why `RiversAndStreams` was free while `SmoothRoads` was not - until LANDFORM3 (2026-10-06, Mac: "Yes rivers should be online") cut painted rivers into the land in the landforms' kernel: it is the room's now, ON, the one key forced past its shipped default, named (`PAST_DEFAULT`), and the count is 47. The classification is TOTAL, every forced key is a declared key at the mod's own shipped default (the port's, for WEAR-VANILLA's three wear keys, which it ships off), the lock/pane/door copy is checked for the two claims that had stopped being true, and the freed modules are still swept for the wire, a save record or a roll. THE SEVENTH PIN CAME FROM A SURVIVING MUTANT that found a hole rather than a missing assertion: dropping `Object.hasOwn` from the lane makes `room['toString']` a FUNCTION handed back as a forced value, and modSettings had the same bug for real - every name on Object.prototype read as a declared switch, so `toString` sailed past the throw and a write of it stored a coerced function. All three doors take own keys now. MODS-ONLINE-3 (Mac: "What part of mod toggle for online do you not understand?") ADDED THE TWO ROWS THAT WERE NOT IN THE MOD STORE. Climates & Calories (`survival`) and Weapon Sheathing (`mwSheathing`) sit in the Mods pane under their authors' names and were still forced online, kept there only because the PORT wrote the code and so carries the switch on the prefs shelf - a distinction that exists nowhere a player can see it. Both pass the same reading: the survival system writes only the entity handed to it (the eighth pin RUNS ten hours of a snowbound night on one body and reads the other back byte-identical), a camp is local, and the one thing that leaves the machine is a corpse's food minted by the KILLER - which is already the owner's word granted to peers, Unleveled Loot's exact shape; and a scabbard is how MY machine DRAWS someone else, which is `peerClassSprites`'s category. The seventh pin makes it total across STORES rather than across mods: every row whose kinds carry `mod` is the player's online, and the one exception - `enhancedEnvironments`, the port's own enhanced outdoors carrying the shared weather's evolution, wearing a mod kind only because Dynamic Skies opens its knobs there - is NAMED, so freeing it is a decision somebody makes rather than a list quietly growing back. MODS-ONLINE-4 (Mac: "What about player balance?") THEN MOVED THREE BACK, and the lesson is in the question: the first three passes all asked "does it desync?" and never asked "does my switch reach another player?". The port IS owner-authoritative, so none of them broke a room - but OWNERSHIP IS NOT PRIVACY, and two of the places a machine owns something are places its answer is handed to everyone else. A world room's layout foes are its HOST's (WORLD2), so the host's Meaner Monsters and PCAAO are what the whole PARTY fights - a host with either off runs a softer dungeon for four people who never chose it and cannot see why. A corpse's pile is granted to peers (WORLD6b-iii(c)) and a room's containers are shared (WORLD4), so Unleveled Loot's rolls do not stay with the roller and two rulesets feed one economy. Climates & Calories was re-asked the same question and KEPT the player's: it reaches another player only through a corpse's FOOD, meat and rations with no power and no gear, and forcing it means nobody may opt out of freezing to death. The lock copy is split, because the three are not locked for the ground's reason and a lock that gives the wrong reason is as useless as no reason. 16 mutants, 16 dead; two older source pins re-aimed (surv2_items, ws1_sheathing) and one stale mutant record re-aimed. MODS-ONLINE-5 (2026-09-23, Mac: "Yeah its the reason we forced PCAAO"): one ruleset per room - RR's six combat overrides forced at the mod's own defaults (the classic bonus off), intensive training forced OFF (the shared clock would hand its +4 out for free), eight of RR's own-run switches pinned free, PCAAO's one-switch precedent, the lane's own words. |
 | crash2_window_contract.test.js | 4 | CRASH2 - THE CONTRACT ITSELF, DERIVED (2026-09-15): the gate CRASH1 shipped, audited one commit later, because the standing rule says the gate IS the fix and so the gate is a claim like any other. It was wrong three ways. F1 - IT READ FIVE DOORS OF EIGHT IN SILENCE: its block finder matched `return {` alone, so `ui/pauseDoor.js` and `ui/inventoryDoor.js` (which name the object first) and `ui/travelMapDoor.js` (which returns classes) were skipped, and the suite went green over a claim covering five of eight doors - one of the two skipped being the pause menu, which is half of what the player reported. F2 - ITS ARM LIST WAS TYPED BY HAND and wrong in both directions: it demanded `close`, which no host has ever called on a slot (the hosts free a window with `dispose?.()`), and omitted `tick`, which `interior.js:"overlay.tick(dt)".."drainOverlay();"` calls unguarded every frame. F3 - IT ASSUMED THE POPULATION WAS `ui/*Door.js`: twelve window classes are constructed straight into a slot, and `townTalk.js:"windows.eachPaintedBeneath"` paints every COVERED window too. So the arms are read off the four window-stack hosts, scoped to the ENCLOSING FUNCTION rather than a fixed lookback (the first pass used four lines and mis-read `townTalk.js:"overlay.hover(v ? v[0] : -1, v ? v[1] : -1, e)"` as unguarded - its guard sits eight lines up; HARD2's D10 pin was re-aimed for the same reason), and `test/windowContract.mjs` holds the one derivation both gates ask. The four tests: the arms derive from the hosts and F2 is pinned in both directions; every DOM door is READ (a door the finder cannot parse is a FAILURE, never a skip - the whole lesson); every DOM door answers input/draw/dispose; and `interior.js` keeps the CLOSED population that lets it alone demand `tick` (six windows in this tree answer no tick, so demanding the union would be a false red six times over). Mutation-verified three ways: restoring CRASH1's narrow finder reddens the cov